{"action":"upsert_decision_section","body":"State, relationships, chronology, and control are distinct properties that have not been jointly validated. Recoverability, residency, behavioral accessibility, and working-set adequacy are conditional on task phase, co-resident set, and regime, not intrinsic [S03]. Semantic compression lowers resident cost but introduces task-dependent loss [S03].

Chronology’s external and resident lifecycle is qualified while healthy. Within a phase, append ordinary model actions and literal results while the next valid packet remains feasible; do not continuously rewrite a prompt that still satisfies the arm's frozen reserves [S09]. History growth causes useful exact evidence to lose co-residency, increasing reopening churn [S03].

Control mechanisms include host-owned response and control reserves, which are earned [S02][S10]. Model-owned eviction is not validated [S02]. The interaction between chronology lifecycle and semantic persistence remains untested in fresh trajectories [S02][S10].","heading":"State, relationships, chronology, and control"}
